  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nCurrently, ridging is centered to our southwest with a closed low \nover the desert southwest. Surface high pressure remain in place \nacross the eastern U.S., leading to continued dry weather. By \nWednesday, ridging will expand in the east as the western closed low \nprogresses into the southern Rockies. Height rises will continue the \nrecent warming trend with most places rising well into the 80s to \nlow 90s in the south. By Thursday, the closed low will eject into \nthe Great Plains, leading to the development of an open wave low \npressure system. Further height rises in the east will push 500mb \nheights to 5,900m or higher over the region, which are above the \n90th percentile for this time of year. When combined with broad \nsoutherly low-level flow, this will make the case for further \ntemperature increases, likely around 90 degrees for most southern \nand central valley areas. \n\nBy Friday, the closed low lift to our north with the surface low \nfollowing the same trend. The frontal boundary from the initial low \nwill gradually approach from the north and reach the area by \nSaturday. The flow, while still weak, will be southerly to \nsouthwesterly, bringing abundant moisture into the region. This is \nevidenced by PWAT values exceeding 1.6 inches, the 90th percentile \nfor this time of year. As the front remains over the area Saturday \nand Sunday, persistent rain can be expected. This will be helpful \nfor ongoing drought conditions in portions of the area, but locally \nheavy or persistent rain could increase potential of localized \nflooding. By early next week, milder and drier conditions are \nexpected as high pressure from the north pushes the front to our \nsouth.",
